{
 "schema": "bucket.solvability-atlas.record/v1",
 "id": "llmreason",
 "title": "Formal reasoning limits of transformers",
 "statement": {
  "text": "In deep learning, the transformer is a family of artificial neural network architectures based on the multi-head attention mechanism, in which input data such as text, images, or audio is converted to a sequence of numerical representations called tokens, and each token is converted into a vector through an embedding layer. At each layer, each token is then contextualized within the scope of the context window with other (unmasked) tokens via a parallel multi-head attention mechanism, allowing the signal for key tokens to be amplified and less important tokens to be diminished. Because self-attention alone is permutation-invariant, transformers inject positional information, typically through positional encodings or learned positional embeddings, so token order can affect the output.",
  "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Transformer_(deep_learning)",
  "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
  "attribution": {
   "title": "Transformer (deep learning)",
   "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Transformer_(deep_learning)"
  }
 },
